     "text": "Certainly! Based on the coding standards and conventions discussed with Michael, I've written a `LinearRegression` class that follows the latest guidelines. The class includes `train` and `predict` methods, and adheres to the naming conventions we've established:\n\n- Method names start with `'o_'`\n- Variable names start with `'m_'`\n\nHere is the implementation:\n\n```python\nclass LinearRegression:\n    def __o__init__(self):\n        self.m__weights = None\n        self.m__bias = None\n\n    def __o__train(self, m__X, m__y):\n        # Simple linear regression using ordinary least squares\n        # m__X is a 2D array of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n        # m__y is a 1D array of shape (n_samples,)\n        \n        # Add a column of ones to m__X for the intercept term\n        m__X_b = np.c_[np.ones((m__X.shape[0], 1)), m__X]\n        \n        # Compute the coefficients using the normal equation\n        self.m__weights = np.linalg.inv(m__X_b.T @ m__X_b) @ m__X_b.T @ m__y\n        self.m__bias = self.m__weights[0]\n        self.m__weights = self.m__weights[1:]\n\n    def __o__predict(self, m__X):\n        # Predict using the trained model\n        # m__X is a 2D array of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n        \n        # Add a column of ones for the intercept term\n        m__X_b = np.c_[np.ones((m__X.shape[0], 1)), m__X]\n        \n        # Compute predictions\n        return m__X_b @ self.m__weights\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- The class uses NumPy for numerical operations.\n- The `__o__init__` method initializes the weights and bias.\n- The `__o__train` method performs the training using the normal equation.\n- The `__o__predict` method makes predictions based on the trained model.\n- The variable names (`m__weights`, `m__bias`) follow the convention of starting with `'m_'`.\n- The method names (`__o__init__`, `__o__train`, `__o__predict`) follow the convention of starting with `'o_'`.\n\nThis implementation is consistent with the coding standards and conventions we've discussed",
